<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bellapais Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bellapais Abbey is a ruined monastery near Kyrenia in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, founded around 1200 and largely rebuilt under the Lusignan kings in the 13th and 14th centuries. Built for Premonstratensian canons, its church, cloister and great refectory are among the finest Gothic buildings in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bellapais-abbey.jpg" alt="Bellapais Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyrenia_-_Girne_Kloster_Bellapa%C3%ADs_Kirche_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site stands at 220 metres above sea level on the northern side of the village of Bellapais, about 5 kilometres from Kyrenia, with a long view down to the sea. Its older name, Episcopia or Piscopia, suggests it may have served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Kyrenia as a residence and a refuge from Arab raids in the 7th and 8th centuries. The first known settlers were Canons Regular of the Holy Sepulchre, who fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> after it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1187. Aimery de Lusignan founded the monastery, consecrated as the Abbey of St. Mary of the Mountain, and the White Canons took it over in 1206.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The main building was raised under King Hugh III (1267–1284), and the cloister and refectory under Hugh IV between 1324 and 1359; Hugh IV lived there in apartments of his own. The Genoese stripped it of everything portable in their raid on Kyrenia in 1373. By the Venetian period the canons had abandoned their rule and reportedly took wives. After the Ottoman conquest of 1571 the abbey was given to the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and fell into disrepair, though its church served the village that grew round it. Under British rule soldiers used the refectory as a hospital from 1878, and George Jeffery, Curator of the Ancient Monuments of Cyprus, repaired the abbey in 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A fortified gate on the south leads to a forecourt and the 13th-century church, the best preserved part, with a nave and two aisles, a carved pulpit and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s throne. The cloister has 18 arches; under one, two Roman sarcophagi served the canons as basins. The Gothic refectory, 30 by 10 metres, has a pulpit projecting from its north wall, a rose window in the east wall and bullet holes left by British soldiers, and its doorway carries the arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the Lusignans. The chapter house keeps a carving of figures, beasts, sirens and a canon. The refectory is now used for concerts and lectures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bellapais Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gothic cathedrals in Nicosia and Famagusta and the abbey of <b>Bellapais</b> survive.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
